      title: "Glaucon's Challenge: The Origin of Justice",
      body: `## Not satisfied

At the start of Book 2, Socrates thinks the discussion is over. "I thought, with these words, that I was done with the argument; but it turned out to be only a prelude" (357a). **Glaucon**, Plato's brother, "who is always most courageous in everything," refuses to accept Thrasymachus's surrender:

> "Socrates, do you want to seem to have persuaded us, or truly to persuade us, that in every way it is better to be just than unjust?" (*Republic* 2.357a–b, plain rendering)

Socrates says he would prefer to truly persuade them. Then, Glaucon says, he has not done so yet.

## Three kinds of goods

Glaucon begins by distinguishing **three kinds of good things** (357b–d):

1. Things we welcome **for their own sake**, not for their consequences: such as **joy**, and harmless pleasures that have no further effects.
2. Things we welcome **both for their own sake and for their consequences**: such as **thinking**, **seeing**, and **being healthy**.
3. Things we welcome **only for their consequences**, which in themselves are burdensome: such as **physical training**, **medical treatment**, and **making money**.

In which class, Glaucon asks, does Socrates place **justice**? Socrates answers: in the **finest** class, the second, of things that are good both in themselves and for their consequences, "which anyone who is going to be blessed must love both for itself and for what comes from it" (358a).

But most people, Glaucon says, put justice in the **third** class: they practice it for the sake of its rewards, reputation and the avoidance of punishment, but regard it in itself as burdensome and to be avoided. This is the view that Glaucon will now defend, not because he believes it, but because he wants to hear Socrates refute it properly. He will praise injustice as strongly as he can, so that Socrates can praise justice in return (358b–d).

Glaucon's challenge sets the task for the rest of the *Republic*. Socrates must show that justice is good **in itself**, apart from its consequences: that the just person is better off, simply by being just, than the unjust person, even if the unjust person gets all the rewards and the just person none.

## The origin of justice

Glaucon begins with an account of the **origin** of justice, which is the earliest surviving statement of what later came to be called the **social contract** theory:

> "They say that by nature, doing injustice is good, and suffering injustice is bad, but that the badness of suffering it far exceeds the goodness of doing it. So when people do injustice to one another and suffer it, and taste both, those who are not able to escape the one and choose the other decide that it is profitable to make an agreement with one another neither to do injustice nor to suffer it. And from this they began to make laws and covenants, and called what the law commanded lawful and just. And this is the origin and essence of justice: it is a mean between the best, which is to do injustice without paying the penalty, and the worst, which is to suffer injustice without being able to take revenge." (*Republic* 2.358e–359a, plain rendering)

On this account, justice is not natural. It is a **compromise**, made by the weak, who cannot protect themselves against the strong. Everyone would prefer to do injustice and get away with it. But since most people cannot, and fear suffering injustice more than they enjoy doing it, they agree to a system of laws that forbids both. Justice is "loved not as a good, but because people are too weak to do injustice." A real man, who could do injustice without being punished, "would never make an agreement with anyone neither to do injustice nor to suffer it; he would be mad to do so" (359b).

## The social contract after Plato

Glaucon's account is the ancestor of a long tradition in political philosophy. **Epicurus**, in the third century BCE, taught that "justice is not something in itself, but a kind of compact, in dealings with one another, not to harm or be harmed" (*Principal Doctrines* 33). In the seventeenth century, the English philosopher **Thomas Hobbes**, in *Leviathan* (1651), argued that in the "state of nature," without government, the life of man would be "solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short," and that people agree to give up their natural liberty to a sovereign in exchange for security. Later contract theorists, such as **John Locke**, **Jean-Jacques Rousseau**, and, in the twentieth century, **John Rawls**, developed different versions of the idea. Rawls, in *A Theory of Justice* (1971), imagined people choosing principles of justice from behind a "**veil of ignorance**," not knowing what position they would occupy in society.

What distinguishes Glaucon's version is its **cynicism**. For Glaucon's imagined speaker, the contract is a second-best arrangement, which the strong would reject if they could. Justice is a **necessary evil**, accepted because we are weak.

## Why the challenge is hard

Glaucon's challenge is much harder to answer than Thrasymachus's. Thrasymachus spoke rudely and inconsistently, and could be tripped up by Socrates's questions. Glaucon presents the case calmly, systematically, and with the clarity of someone who is not committed to it. He grants that justice may be **useful**, as a social arrangement that protects the weak. What he denies is that it is **good in itself**, for the person who has it. Socrates cannot answer him simply by showing that justice has good consequences, since Glaucon has already conceded this. He must show that justice benefits the soul **directly**, whatever its consequences.

> **Pause and consider.** Glaucon says that justice is a compromise: people would rather do injustice and get away with it, but agree to laws because they fear suffering it more. Is this how morality began? If it is, does that make morality any less binding?`,
