      body: `## The thought experiment

To show that people are just only **unwillingly**, Glaucon tells a story. Suppose, he says, we gave both the just and the unjust man the power to do whatever they wanted, and watched where their desires led them. We would catch the just man going the same way as the unjust, "because of the desire to get more (*pleonexia*), which every nature naturally pursues as good, but which is forced by law to honor equality" (359c).

The power he has in mind is that of the **Ring of Gyges**:

> "They say that Gyges was a shepherd in the service of the ruler of Lydia at that time. There was a great storm and an earthquake, and the earth split open, and a chasm appeared at the place where he was pasturing. He saw it and wondered, and went down into it. And there, the story goes, among other wonders, he saw a hollow bronze horse, with little doors; and stooping to look in, he saw inside a corpse, larger than human size, as it seemed, with nothing on it except a gold ring on its hand. He took the ring and went out." (*Republic* 2.359d–e, plain rendering)

At the monthly meeting of the shepherds, to report to the king on the flocks, Gyges was sitting among the others when he happened to turn the **collet** (the setting) of the ring toward himself, into the palm of his hand. At once he became **invisible** to those sitting around him, who spoke of him as if he had gone away. He turned the collet outward again, and became visible. He tested the ring again and again, and found that it always had this power. Then he arranged to be one of the messengers sent to the king, and when he arrived, he **seduced the queen**, with her help **killed the king**, and **seized the throne** (359e–360b).

The name **Gyges** belongs to a historical king of **Lydia**, who ruled in the early seventh century BCE, and founded the dynasty that ended with Croesus. Herodotus tells a different story of his rise to power: the king **Candaules**, proud of his wife's beauty, forced his bodyguard Gyges to hide in the royal bedroom and see her naked; the queen saw him, and gave Gyges a choice: kill the king and marry her, or die (Herodotus 1.8–13). Plato's version replaces the hiding behind a door with a magic ring; in the text, the shepherd is called Gyges's **ancestor**, though most translations simplify this. Both stories are about seeing without being seen, and the power that comes from it.

## The two rings

Now, Glaucon says, suppose there were **two** such rings, and the just man put on one, and the unjust man the other.

> "No one, it seems, would be so adamant as to remain in justice and have the strength to keep away from what belongs to others and not touch it, when he could take whatever he wanted from the marketplace without fear, and go into houses and have sex with whomever he wished, and kill or release from prison whomever he wished, and do other things as an equal to a god among men. … And this is a great proof, one might say, that no one is just willingly, but only under compulsion, since justice is not a good for him privately." (*Republic* 2.360b–c, plain rendering)

Anyone who had such power and refused to use it, Glaucon adds, would be thought by others to be "most wretched and most foolish," though they would praise him to each other's faces, deceiving one another, for fear of suffering injustice (360d).

## The two lives

Glaucon then makes his challenge sharper still. To judge which life is better, we must compare the **perfectly unjust** man and the **perfectly just** man, each in his extreme form (360e–362c).

The perfectly unjust man must be like a skilled craftsman, who knows what is possible and what is not, and corrects his mistakes. He must commit the greatest injustices while having the **greatest reputation for justice**, "for the extreme of injustice is to seem just when one is not." If he is caught, he must be able to defend himself with persuasive speech, or by force, with courage, strength, wealth, and friends.

The perfectly just man, by contrast, must be "a simple and noble man, who, as Aeschylus says, wishes not to seem but to be good." We must **strip him of everything except justice**. He must have the **greatest reputation for injustice**, though he does no wrong, so that his justice can be tested, to see whether he is just for the sake of justice or for the sake of reputation and its rewards. And he must remain unshaken until death:

> "They will say that the just man, being such, will be whipped, racked, bound, have his eyes burned out, and finally, after suffering every evil, will be impaled, and will learn that one should wish not to be, but to seem just." (*Republic* 2.361e–362a, plain rendering)

Meanwhile, the unjust man, with his reputation for justice, will rule in the city, marry whom he wishes, make profitable contracts, defeat his enemies, grow rich, help his friends, and offer magnificent sacrifices to the gods, so that he will be dearer to the gods than the just man (362b–c).

This is the challenge. Socrates must show that the just man, flogged, tortured, and impaled, with a reputation for wickedness, is **happier** than the unjust man, ruling the city, rich, honored, and loved by the gods.

## The just man crucified

The word translated "impaled" (*anaschindyleuthēsetai*) refers to a form of execution in which the victim was fastened to a stake or plank; some translators render it "crucified." Christian readers, from the early centuries onward, were struck by the resemblance between Glaucon's perfectly just man, who has a reputation for injustice, suffers every torment, and is finally fastened to a stake, and the figure of Jesus. Clement of Alexandria noted the passage (*Stromateis* 5.14). The resemblance is a coincidence of history, since Plato wrote four centuries before Jesus; but it shows how deeply Plato's question about the suffering of the just touches the human imagination. The prophet Isaiah, writing centuries before Plato, had described the servant of the Lord who "was despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief" (Isaiah 53:3, King James Version).

## The question of invisibility

The Ring of Gyges is one of the most famous **thought experiments** in philosophy. It asks a simple question: **would you be just if you could never be caught?** If not, then you value justice only for its consequences, for the reputation and safety it brings. Glaucon believes that most people, if they were honest, would admit that they would not be just. Socrates must show that a person who understood what justice is would choose it even with the ring on his finger. The idea has been retold many times, from the invisible man of **H. G. Wells** (1897) to J. R. R. Tolkien's ring in *The Lord of the Rings*, which, like Gyges's, corrupts those who wear it.

> **Pause and consider.** If you had the Ring of Gyges and could never be caught, would you still be just? Be honest. What does your answer suggest about why you are just now?`,
