      body: `## The brother's speech

Glaucon has finished, and Socrates is about to reply, when **Adeimantus**, Plato's other brother, intervenes. Glaucon, he says, has left out the most important point (362d). Glaucon described what the defenders of injustice say. Adeimantus will describe what the **defenders of justice** say, and show that they too praise justice only for its **consequences**.

## What fathers say

Fathers, Adeimantus says, tell their sons that they must be just. But they do not praise justice itself; they praise the **reputation** for justice, and the good things that come from it: offices, marriages, and all the advantages that Glaucon listed for the unjust man with a reputation for justice (362e–363a).

The poets go further. **Hesiod** and **Homer** say that the gods reward the just with rich harvests, oaks laden with acorns and bees, sheep heavy with wool, and fertile wives. **Musaeus** and his son promise even better rewards in the afterlife: the just will lie at a feast, garlanded, drunk forever, "as if the finest reward of virtue were eternal drunkenness." The unjust are buried in mud in Hades, or made to carry water in a sieve (363a–e).

So, Adeimantus points out, even those who **praise** justice praise it only for its **rewards**, in this life or the next. No one praises it for itself.

## Justice is hard, injustice pleasant

Moreover, people commonly say that justice is **hard** and laborious, and that injustice is **pleasant** and easy, shameful only by reputation and law. They say that injustice is usually more profitable than justice. They are ready to call the wicked happy if they are rich and powerful, and to despise the poor and weak, though they admit they are better (364a–b).

## The gods can be bought

Worst of all, Adeimantus says, is what people say about the **gods**. They say that the gods often give misfortune and a wretched life to good people, and the opposite to bad people. And **begging priests** and **soothsayers** go to the doors of the rich, persuading them that they have a power, given by the gods, to make amends for any wrong they or their ancestors have done, by sacrifices and charms, with pleasures and feasts; and that, for a small fee, they can harm an enemy, just or unjust, by spells and binding curses. They cite Homer, who says that the gods can be turned aside by prayers and sacrifices (*Iliad* 9.497–501). They produce books by **Orpheus** and **Musaeus**, according to which they perform rites, persuading not only individuals but whole cities that there are **purifications** for wrongdoing, through sacrifices and games, both for the living and the dead, which free us from evils in the afterlife (364b–365a).

What, Adeimantus asks, will a young man conclude from all this, if he is clever and able to draw conclusions? He will think, with Pindar: "Shall I climb the high wall by justice or by crooked deceit?" (365b). Since, as the wise say, "seeming overpowers truth," he will devote himself to seeming just. He will draw around himself a "**façade**" of virtue, while trailing behind him "the cunning and crafty fox" of the poet Archilochus (365c). If someone objects that it is hard to stay hidden, he will answer that nothing great is easy, and that there are secret societies and political clubs, and teachers of persuasion, to help.

And what about the **gods**? Either they do not exist, or they do not care about human affairs, in which case we need not care about hiding from them. Or they exist and care, but, as the poets themselves tell us, they can be **persuaded** by sacrifices and prayers. So we should do wrong, and then sacrifice from the proceeds. "If we are just, we will be unpunished by the gods, but we will give up the profits of injustice; if we are unjust, we will make a profit, and, by praying when we transgress and err, we will persuade them and escape unpunished" (365d–366a).

## The demand

Adeimantus concludes with a direct appeal to Socrates:

> "So do not only show us by argument that justice is better than injustice, but show us what each of them does to the person who has it, in itself, whether it is hidden from gods and men or not, so that one is good and the other bad. … Leave out reputations, as Glaucon urged. … Show us what each does, by itself, to the person who has it, that makes the one good and the other bad." (*Republic* 2.367b–e, plain rendering)

This is the clearest statement of the task. Socrates must show what justice and injustice **do to the soul** of the person who has them, regardless of reputation or rewards. He must show that justice is a good **like health**, which benefits its possessor directly, and injustice an evil **like disease**.

## Two brothers

Glaucon and Adeimantus are Plato's brothers. By giving them these speeches, Plato honors them: they are presented as young men of exceptional ability, who can state the case for injustice better than its defenders, yet who are not persuaded by it. Socrates praises them, quoting a line of poetry written about them by a lover of Glaucon's, after they distinguished themselves at the battle of Megara: "Sons of Ariston, godlike offspring of a famous man" (368a). "There must be something truly divine in you," Socrates says, "if you are not persuaded that injustice is better than justice, though you can speak so well on its behalf."

The challenge of Glaucon and Adeimantus is the most important passage in the *Republic*, because it defines what the rest of the work must do. Many readers have thought that the *Republic* never fully answers it. Whether it does is one of the questions you will need to decide.

> **Pause and consider.** Adeimantus points out that even those who praise justice, parents and poets, praise it only for its rewards. Is this true of the moral education you received? Were you taught to be good for its own sake, or for what it would bring you?`,
