      body: `## Larger letters

Socrates admits that the task is difficult, and that he is not sure he is equal to it. But he proposes a method. Suppose, he says, that people with poor eyesight were asked to read small letters from a distance, and then someone noticed that the same letters were written **larger** somewhere else. Wouldn't it be a godsend to read the larger letters first, and then compare them with the smaller (368c–d)?

Justice, Socrates says, is found both in a single **individual** and in a whole **city**. A city is larger than an individual. So the justice in a city may be **easier to see**. "So, if you are willing, let us first investigate what justice is in cities; and then let us examine it in the individual, looking for the likeness of the greater in the form of the less" (368e–369a). And if we watch a city **coming into being** in speech, perhaps we will see justice and injustice coming into being in it.

This is the famous **city-soul analogy**, which structures the whole *Republic*. It assumes that the justice of a city and the justice of a soul are the **same thing**, differing only in scale. This assumption has been much criticized, and we will return to it in lesson 3. But it allows Socrates to turn the discussion of justice into a discussion of **politics**, and to build, in imagination, an ideal city.

## The first city

Why do cities come into being? Because "none of us is self-sufficient, but each needs many things" (369b). Our first and greatest need is **food**; then **housing**; then **clothing**. So the smallest city would have a farmer, a builder, a weaver, and perhaps a shoemaker and someone to care for the body.

Should each of them produce everything for himself, or should each do one job for all? Socrates argues for the **division of labor**: "we are not all born alike, but differ in nature, one suited to one task, another to another" (370a–b). More and better work is done when each person does **one** thing, according to his nature, at the right time. So the farmer will not make his own plow; there will be carpenters, smiths, herdsmen, merchants to import what the city cannot produce, sailors, shopkeepers, and wage laborers (370c–371e).

The principle that each person should do the **one** job for which he is naturally suited, introduced here as a matter of economic efficiency, will turn out, in Book 4, to be the definition of **justice** itself.

## The city of pigs

Socrates describes the life of this simple city:

> "They will make bread and wine and clothes and shoes, and build houses. … They will feed themselves on barley and wheat, baking cakes and loaves and setting them out on reeds or clean leaves; and reclining on couches strewn with yew and myrtle, they will feast with their children, drinking wine, crowned with garlands, and singing hymns to the gods, living pleasantly with one another, not having more children than their means allow, guarding against poverty or war." (*Republic* 2.372a–c, plain rendering)

**Glaucon** objects. "You are feasting these men without any **relishes**," he says. Socrates adds salt, olives, cheese, onions, greens, figs, chickpeas, beans, myrtle berries, and acorns roasted in the fire. Glaucon is not satisfied: "If you were founding a **city of pigs**, Socrates, what else would you feed them?" (372c–d). He wants couches and tables, and the delicacies that people have nowadays.

Socrates understands. "We are examining not only how a city comes into being, but a **luxurious** city." And he adds a remarkable judgment: "The true city, in my opinion, is the one we have described, the **healthy** one, as it were. But if you want, let us look at a **feverish** city too" (372e).

## The feverish city

The luxurious city needs far more: couches, tables, perfumes, incense, courtesans, cakes, painting, embroidery, gold, ivory; hunters, artists, poets, rhapsodes, actors, dancers, makers of women's ornaments, servants, nurses, hairdressers, barbers, cooks, swineherds; and **doctors**, many more than before, because of the unhealthy way of life (373a–d).

And the land that was enough for the healthy city will no longer be enough. "Then we must cut off a slice of our neighbors' land, if we are to have enough for pasture and plowing; and they in turn will want ours, if they too give themselves over to the unlimited acquisition of money, going beyond the boundary of the necessary." And so, Socrates says, "we shall go to **war**, Glaucon" (373d–e). War, he adds, without saying yet whether it does good or harm, has its origin in the same things from which come the greatest evils for cities, both private and public: the desire for **more**.

## The guardians

A city that goes to war needs an **army**. And, by the principle of the division of labor, the army must be a separate, specialized class of professional soldiers, the **guardians** (*phylakes*), whose only job is to protect the city (374a–e). This is a striking departure from Athenian practice, where every citizen was a soldier.

What kind of nature should the guardians have? They must be like **noble dogs**: quick to perceive, swift, strong, and **spirited** (*thumoeidēs*) toward enemies, but **gentle** to their own people. But how can one nature be both fierce and gentle? Socrates points to the dog, which is gentle to those it knows and fierce to strangers, and so "loves learning," since it distinguishes friend from enemy by knowledge alone. So the guardian must be by nature **philosophical** as well as spirited (375a–376c).

## Why the luxurious city?

Why does Socrates build the luxurious city, when he thinks the simple one is the "true" and "healthy" city? Perhaps because Glaucon, and most people, would not accept the simple life; and because justice and injustice, Socrates says, may be found in the way they grow in a luxurious city (372e). The rest of the *Republic* is an attempt to **purge** the feverish city, by education and discipline, until it becomes healthy again. Some readers have thought that the "city of pigs" is Plato's real ideal, a picture of simple, peaceful, natural life; others that it is too simple to contain philosophy, or the virtues, which require the challenge of desire.

> **Pause and consider.** Socrates calls the simple city "healthy" and the luxurious city "feverish," and says that war comes from the desire for more than is necessary. Is he right? Can a society enjoy luxury without becoming feverish?`,
