      title: "The Education of the Guardians: Stories About the Gods",
      body: `## Music and gymnastics

How should the guardians be educated? Socrates proposes the traditional Greek education: **gymnastics** (*gymnastikē*) for the body, and **music** (*mousikē*) for the soul (376e). The Greek word *mousikē* means much more than "music" in our sense. It means all the arts of the **Muses**: poetry, music, dance, and the stories, myths, and literature in which young Greeks were raised. And it comes **first**, before gymnastics, because children hear stories before they are old enough for physical training.

## The power of stories

Socrates argues that the stories children hear shape their souls profoundly, because the young are impressionable, and receive whatever "**stamp**" one wishes to give them (377a–b). So the founders of the city must **supervise** the makers of stories, accepting the good ones and rejecting the bad, and persuade nurses and mothers to tell their children only the approved stories, "shaping their souls with stories far more than their bodies with their hands" (377c).

What stories must be rejected? Most of the stories told by **Homer** and **Hesiod**, the poets on whom every Greek was raised. Socrates is especially concerned with stories that tell **lies** about the gods, "and lie badly," which is the greatest lie about the greatest things (377d–e). The worst is Hesiod's story of how **Ouranos** was castrated by his son **Kronos**, and Kronos overthrown by **Zeus** (*Theogony* 154–210, 453–506), which you may have read in APH 101. Even if such stories were true, they should not be told to the young, who might think it no great thing to punish a father who has wronged them. If they must be told at all, it should be to as few as possible, in secret, after a sacrifice, not of a pig, but of something great and hard to obtain (378a).

Nor should the young hear stories of gods **warring** with gods, or plotting against one another, or the battles of the giants, or the binding of Hera by her son, or Hephaestus being thrown from heaven by his father, or the battles of the gods in Homer, "whether they are allegories or not; for the young cannot judge what is allegory and what is not" (378b–e).

## The patterns of theology

Instead of particular stories, Socrates lays down **patterns** (*typoi*) or rules for how the gods must be represented, the first explicit **theology** in Greek literature. The word *theologia*, "speech about the gods," appears here for the first time in surviving Greek (379a). Socrates sets out two laws:

**First law: God is the cause only of good.** God is truly **good**, and what is good cannot be harmful, and what is not harmful does no harm, and so is not the cause of any evil. "So God, since he is good, is not the cause of all things, as the many say, but of a few things for human beings, and not the cause of most; for good things are far fewer than bad for us. And for the good things, no one else must be held responsible; but for the bad things, one must look for some other causes, not God" (379c). So the poets must not say, as Homer does, that "two jars stand on the floor of Zeus, one full of good fates, the other of bad" (*Iliad* 24.527–528). If the gods punish people, the poet must say that the punishment benefits them (380a–b).

**Second law: God does not change or deceive.** What is in the best condition is least changed by outside influences. God is perfect, so he cannot be changed by anything else; and he would not change himself, since he could change only for the worse. So the gods do not take on different shapes, as the poets say, disguising themselves as strangers and wandering through cities (*Odyssey* 17.485–486). Nor do they **lie**, in word or deed, or send false dreams and signs, as Zeus sends a lying dream to Agamemnon in the *Iliad* (2.1–34) (380d–383a).

## Xenophanes again

These laws recall the critique of Homer and Hesiod by **Xenophanes**, which you may have studied in APH 101. Xenophanes had complained that the poets attributed to the gods "all things that are shameful among men," and had proposed a single god, unlike mortals, who does not move or change. Plato goes further: he not only criticizes the poets, but proposes that their stories be **banned** from the education of the young.

## Censorship

Socrates's proposals amount to **censorship**: the state will control what stories may be told, and suppress those that are harmful. This has made the *Republic* notorious. **Karl Popper**, in *The Open Society and Its Enemies* (1945), written during the Second World War, attacked Plato as a forerunner of **totalitarianism**, who would subordinate art, truth, and individual freedom to the interests of the state. Others have defended Plato, pointing out that his concern is with the **education of children**, not the censorship of adult art, and that every society makes choices about what to teach the young. Modern debates about children's literature, school curricula, violent video games, and the influence of media on the young continue Plato's argument, whatever position one takes.

What is new in Plato is not that he wants to protect children, but that he applies **moral** and **philosophical** standards to the poetry that was, for the Greeks, the foundation of their culture and religion. To criticize Homer, as Plato does, was a bit like a modern writer proposing to censor the Bible or Shakespeare.

## Truth and falsehood

Socrates also makes an interesting distinction between two kinds of **lies** (382a–d). The "**true lie**," falsehood in the soul about the most important things, is hated by gods and men; no one willingly wishes to be deceived in his own soul about what is real. But a **lie in words**, an imitation of the falsehood in the soul, is sometimes useful: against enemies; to prevent a friend from doing harm, in madness or folly; and in the stories we tell about ancient times, where we do not know the truth, making the falsehood as much like the truth as possible. This distinction prepares the way for the **noble lie** of Book 3.

> **Pause and consider.** Socrates proposes that the stories told to children should present the gods as good and unchanging, and that stories of divine violence should be banned. Should a society control the stories its children hear? Who should decide which stories are harmful?`,
